WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.590
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:09.490
In this piece of work, we seek to control
digital characters in real-time.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:14.490
It happens the following way: we specify a
target trajectory, and the algorithm has to

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:18.380
synthesize a series of motions that follows
that path.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.660
To make these motions as realistic as possible,
this is typically accomplished by unleashing

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:29.980
a learning algorithm on a large database that
contains a ton of motion information.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.620
Previous techniques did not have a good understanding
of these databases and they often synthesized

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.570
motions from pieces that corresponded to different
kinds of movements.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:44.340
This lack of understanding results in stiff,
unnatural output motion.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:49.160
Intuitively, it is a bit like putting together
a sentence from a set of letters that were

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.480
cut out one by one from different newspaper
articles.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:58.550
It is a fully formed sentence, but it lacks
the smoothness and the flow of a properly

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.540
aligned piece of text.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:05.080
This is a neural network based technique that
introduces a phase function to the learning

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.190
process.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:10.979
This phase function augments the learning
with the timing information of a given motion.

00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:16.490
With this phase function, the neural network
recognizes that we are not only learning periodic

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:20.960
motions, but it knows when these motions start
and when they end.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.570
The final technique takes very little memory,
runs in real time, and it accomplishes smooth

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:34.020
walking, running, jumping and climbing motions
and so much more over a variety of terrains

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.009
with flying colors.

00:01:36.009 --> 00:01:40.340
In a previous episode, we have discussed a
different technique that accomplished something

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:43.820
similar with a low and high level controller.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:48.950
One of the major selling points of this technique
is that this one offers a unified solution

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:53.440
for terrain traversal with using only one
neural network.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.509
This has the potential to make it really big
on computer games and real-time animation.

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:03.090
It is absolutely amazing to witness this and
be a part of the future.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:07.360
Make sure to have a look at the paper, which
also contains the details of a terrain fitting

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.580
step to make this learning algorithm capable
of taking into consideration a variety of

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:13.580
obstacles.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:18.970
I would also like to thank Claudio Pannacci
for his amazing work in translating so many

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.110
of these episodes to Italian.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:25.100
This makes Two Minute Papers accessible for
more people around the globe, and the more

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.600
people we can reach, the happier I am.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.080
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

